ce_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "timestamp": "2026-10-28T22:13:50.107Z", "request_id": "req_011CgVQQLeDserkJpwfYmUKr", "thinking_duration_ms": 24062}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_stop", "index": 0}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "stream_event", "event": {"type": "content_block_start", "index": 1, "content_block": {"type": "text", "text": ""}}, "parent_tool_use_id": null}, {"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgVQQM6FqyEyHoBwjkHz1", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I ranked the plans by working through the rules mentally on the forecast values. I didn't simulate day by day, so these probabilities are estimates.\n\n**What each plan does.** Plans 1, 3, 4 and 5 order every day. Plans 2, 6 and 7 order every other day. Plan 0 orders every three days. Plan 1 orders exactly the forecast demand for its arrival day. Plan 5 orders about 10% above the forecast and plan 3 about 20% above. Plan 4 orders about 25% above for product 0 and about 5% below for product 1. In the every-other-day group, plans 2, 6 and 7 cover two days at about 1.0×, 1.1× and 1.2× the forecast. Plan 0 orders about 1.05–1.1× the forecast for three days at a time.\n\n**Fixed costs: order fees plus extra storage from ordering in batches.**\n\n| Order frequency | Order fees | Extra storage | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Daily (27 orders) | 27 × 49 = 1323 | 0 | about 1323 |\n| Every other day (14 orders) | 14 × 49 = 686 | about 470 | about 1156 |\n| Every three days (9 orders) | 9 × 49 = 441 | about 1000 | about 1441 |\n\nPlan 0 also risks overflowing the 208-unit warehouse when a large delivery arrives (for example, the 66 + 124 delivery on day 18 plus leftovers).\n\n**Order size.** The demand multiplier averages about 1.046, so ordering exactly